From 9f263fcfb5733ec4f33aa8201be4870b635a91ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hoveychen <1111905+hoveychen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 02:18:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] the root containers are as tall as what they hold, in
 standards mode

`getComputedStyle(body).height` was a per-document constant: the viewport less
the body's margins, whatever the document held. 184px in a 200px frame for an
empty body, for one holding a 20px div and for one holding a 900px div alike.
That reading travels -- the Cloudflare widget dumps a whole computed style off
the <body> -- and on example.com Chrome answers 98.0781px where this answered
756px.

Filling the viewport is the QUIRKS rendering, and it stays: an about:blank
document is in quirks mode, which is the one the 0x0-iframe assertions read. In
standards mode nothing sizes the two containers in `dimensionsAt` any more, so
they fall through to the same content pass every other `auto`-height block goes
through. The quirks fill becomes a floor rather than an answer for the same
reason, which is `@max(dims.height, content)` -- a quirks body holding a 900px
div reports 900 of offsetHeight and not 184, and its root grows with it.

Two rules came out of the measurement beside it:

  * a first child's margin cannot escape through the ROOT element, because the
    root's own margins do not collapse (CSS 2.1 8.3.1). The body's 8px UA
    margins are therefore inside the root's content box, which is the whole of
    the 16px between the root's height and the body's;
  * scrollHeight on the SCROLLING ELEMENT is the scrolling AREA and not its own
    box: `max(viewport, the root's border box)`. Which element that is takes the
    same CSSOM swap clientHeight already took -- the root in standards mode, the
    body in quirks -- so one branch answers 200 and 430 for a standards root and
    200 and 916 for a quirks body.

frames/root_box_heights.html pins all of it: nineteen served documents read on a
headed Chrome 151 at dpr 2, eighteen of the nineteen rows byte-identical now
where the whole table disagreed before. The nineteenth is left as the number this
engine answers, with Chrome's beside it -- a descendant's ESCAPED margin still
vanishes instead of collapsing into its ancestor's content height, which
scratch/b73/expr-escaped-margin.js shows is a margin-collapse defect and not a
root-container one (both arms agree at one, two and three levels of plain
nesting; they part on a body holding four such wrappers, 220 against 130).

The two assertions that pinned the old behaviour are re-stated: the top-level
block in frames/iframe_root_box.html said so in its own comment, and the closing
lines of element/position.html called the root containers' size synthetic.
